Here's a summary of what was done in `pipeline_refactored.py`:

**Per-requirement checklist:**

| Requirement | How addressed |
|---|---|
| Environment variables for config | `Config.from_env()` reads `LOG_PATH` and `DB_PATH` from env, falls back to `server.log` / `metrics.db` |
| Fix SQL injection | All inserts use `conn.executemany("INSERT INTO ... VALUES (?, ?, ?)", params)` — parameterized, no string formatting of user data |
| ETL structure | `extract_logs()` → `aggregate_errors()` / `compute_api_latency()` / `track_active_sessions()` → `_load_database()` + `_write_report()` — three clearly separated phases |
| Regex for log parsing | `LINE_PATTERN` (timestamp + level + message), `USER_PATTERN` ("User \<id\> \<action\>"), `API_PATTERN` ("API \<endpoint\> took \<N\>ms") — all named capture groups |
| Type hints + docstrings | Every function has typed params/return and a docstring. `Config` and `LogEvent` are `@dataclass` classes |
| Same report.html output | `diff` confirms identical output |

**Additional improvements:**
- `pathlib.Path` over `os.path`
- Context managers (`with log_path.open()`, `with conn`) — no bare `open`/`close`
- No dead PostgreSQL credentials (original had `DB_HOST`/`DB_PORT`/`DB_USER`/`DB_PASS` that were never used for SQLite)
- `ruff check` passes clean
- Each function stays well under the 30-line / complexity-10 budget
